.TH PUT 1 2026-11-01 "put 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
put, put.raw, put.lines, put.nul, put.peek, put.quote, put.json, put.help, put.version \- print arguments exactly as given
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B put
.RI [ arg ...]
.br
.B put.\c
.I mode
.RI [ arg ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B put
prints its arguments literally.
It has no options and interprets no escape sequences, so
.B put
"$x" prints the value of
.I x
whatever it contains, including
.BR \-n ,
.B \-\-
or backslashes.
.PP
One binary is installed under several names.
The name it is run as selects the mode:
.TP
.B put
Arguments joined by single spaces, then a newline.
With no arguments, prints a newline.
.TP
.B put.raw
Arguments joined by single spaces, with no trailing newline.
.TP
.B put.lines
Each argument followed by a newline.
.TP
.B put.nul
Each argument followed by a NUL byte, for
.BR "xargs \-0" .
.TP
.B put.peek
A header with the number of arguments, then a numbered line per argument
showing what it really contains:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
leading and trailing spaces as \(pc;
.IP \(bu
an empty argument as \(es empty;
.IP \(bu
tab, newline, carriage return and escape as \et, \en, \er and \ee, and
other control bytes as \ex07 and the like;
.IP \(bu
bytes that are not valid UTF-8 as \exff and the like;
.IP \(bu
every character that renders as nothing, as blank space, or depending on
the font as \eu{a0} and the like: format characters (zero-width and
bidirectional controls, tags), spaces other than U+0020, line and paragraph
separators, default-ignorable characters such as variation selectors,
unassigned and private-use code points, and a combining mark with no
character before it to attach to.
.RE
.IP
On a terminal, escapes and markers are colored, and bidirectional controls
are shown in red.
Otherwise there is no color; instead a literal backslash is shown as \e\e
and a literal \(pc or \(es is escaped, so plain output is lossless: each
argument can be recovered exactly from its line.
.B put.peek
never writes a control byte or an invisible character taken from its
input, so it is safe to run on untrusted data.
.TP
.B put.quote
The arguments as shell input that recreates them in bash (including 3.2)
and zsh, whether pasted into a script or an interactive shell.
Words made only of letters, digits and
.B _\-./,:+@%
are left bare; other printable text is single-quoted.
Text with control bytes, invalid UTF-8, invisible characters or a stray
combining mark uses $'...' with \exHH byte escapes, so the line shows what
is really there.
Inside $'...', a single quote is written as \ex27, never as \e\(aq, which
bash 3.2 misreads during history expansion.
.TP
.B put.json
The arguments as a JSON array of strings (RFC 8259).
Control characters, U+2028 and U+2029 are written as \eu escapes.
.TP
.B put.help
A summary of the modes.
.TP
.B put.version
The version and the Unicode version its character tables come from, as
.BR "put 0.1.0 (Unicode 17.0)" .
.PP
Every mode ignores its arguments' meaning entirely: in
.BR "put.version \-\-help" ,
.B \-\-help
is just an unused argument.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Writing to standard output failed.
A message naming the reason goes to standard error.
.TP
.B 2
Run under a name that is not
.B put
or a known
.BI put. mode\fR.
.TP
.B 3
.B put.json
was given an argument that is not valid UTF-8.
Nothing is written.
.PP
If the reader of standard output has gone away,
.B put
is ended by SIGPIPE, like other Unix tools, even if its parent ignored or
blocked that signal.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set and not empty,
.B put.peek
does not use color.
.TP
.B TERM
If
.BR dumb ,
.B put.peek
does not use color.
.PP
No other mode reads the environment, and no mode depends on the locale.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ x=\-n; put "$x"
\-n
$ put.peek "a " $'b\er' "" $'nb\exc2\exa0sp'
4 args
0  a\(pc
1  b\er
2  \(es empty
3  nb\eu{a0}sp
$ put.quote it\e's $'tab\ethere' a=b
\(aqit\(aq\e\(aq\(aqs\(aq $\(aqtab\ethere\(aq \(aqa=b\(aq
$ put.json "say \e"hi\e""
["say \e"hi\e""]
.fi
.SH CAVEATS
.B put.peek
writes UTF-8 and assumes the terminal displays it.
.PP
A terminal that reorders right-to-left text (such as Arabic or Hebrew) can
still move characters around within a line.
Bidirectional control characters are always escaped, but the letters
themselves are shown as they are.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR echo (1),
.BR printf (1),
.BR xargs (1)
